ll  these nodes and they're connected to  everybody else that world is a world  just as relevant to foreign policy as  the traditional chessboard so the book  is really about here's what the world  looks like from the chessboard  perspective here's what it looks like  from the web perspective we need both  but and this is the heart of the book we  need a set of tools to fight Isis or to  combat climate change or to think about  how to stimulate entrepreneurship across  in countries that have no really lively  economy or literacy or health we need  tools and those tools are networks and  so the book is really about here's how  to create networks that will help us  achieve goals in the web world and the  foreign policy makers of the future have  to operate both on the chess board and  in the web okay so what gave you the  idea to do the book and I have wanted to  do this book for a long time certainly  since I came out of government and part  of it is really born of my experience in  government working for Secretary Clinton  and one of the things Secretary Clinton  said was diplomacy and development are  equal pillars of our foreign policy  so yes it's important to try to  negotiate a deal with Iran no question  but it's equally important to ensure  that women's rights are protected around  the world that we have strong health  systems so we don't have an Ebola like  crisis to fight terrorism and  trafficking and trafficking in humans  trafficking and drugs trafficking in  arms but what was so striking was again  on that development side we have foreign  assistance programs we give money to  governments and some of them are corrupt  and even if they spend the money on  their people you don't know quite what  they're doing we had no tools for how to  think about those global problems I  couldn't go into Secretary Clinton and  say we should create X and that will  help solve the problem when it stays  to state I can go in and say we should  sanction the state or we should reach an  agreement with this state I didn't have  anything comparable to that on the  development side so this I have long  wanted to write this book and the other  thing I'll say is we live in a world of  networks I live on social media often  and I just felt like none of that was  part of what we study in international  relations and how are you doing the  research for the book backwards I mean  in the sense that I I said one of the  things interesting about this book is I  have collected books in physics books in  economics in sociology in industrial  organization in psychology in technology  and then more recently lots of works on  things like the mesh economy and and  where Silicon Valley's going because  that part of the US economy is all about  networks too so I've collected all these  books and what I've done now is to go  through enough so that I'm giving a set  of lectures I'll now go back with sort  of it's like building the scaffolding of  a house you it takes a while to design  it you have to do a lot of prep to  design it you then once it's up then you  fill it in that's where I so let's get  to the meat of the book give us some  examples of you know some of these 21st  century problems that are affecting  foreign policy well so let me give you a  couple the first one let's think about  Ukraine so Ukraine the Russia invaded  Ukraine 18 months ago took essentially  took over the Crimea and then has been  been destabilizing eastern Ukraine and  this has caused great diplomatic  conflict between Russia and the EU and  the US Russia on one hand the EU and us  in the other now we have lots of chess  board ways of addressing that issue  we've sanctioned Russia we moved troops  into the Baltic States we've supplied  military aid to the Ukrainian government  we've tried to diplomatically isolate  Russia all good and I'm that's the  chessboard  but what what's  here's the Ukrainian people are  desperate for a better life this is the  second revolution they've had they had  one in 2007 they they're on the border  with Poland they can see countries and  governments that are functioning much  better than theirs they're their  governments totally corrupt their  economy stagnant how are we gonna  actually help them achieve the economy  and the government that they want and  that's where the networks come in so I  talked about well first of all let's  build defense networks to make them more  resilient to continued Russian kind of  harassment I mean it's not because  Russia's not marching troops over the  border Russia's infiltrating people and  so I talk about how you can build  defensive networks that make it harder  for Russia actually to destabilize and  then let's think about stabilization and  assistance networks to help not just the  Ukrainian government but there are a  whole class of young digitally savvy  Ukrainians who want startups who want to  build their economy how do we build the  networks that tie them to the right  people in Europe and the United States  and support them so that's one sort of  set of examples another would be let's  say we have a great solution let's say  we know that in one you know one country  this program for teaching kids to read  has worked brilliantly we know that but  it's helped maybe a thousand kids there  are millions of children around the  world need to do that so we need to  figure out how you create a network that  will replicate that program in the same  way if you think of a a that's a classic  example there are there's alcohol AAA  groups right all over the world and they  replicate because somebody wants to  create one and there's a template to do  it or TEDx right these are people love  TED Talks and they can create their own  TEDx conferences so I talked about the  structure of those networks if you  create a template and you create the  right set of incentives you can get a  replication Network so all different  kinds  networks it seems a little odd now  because we haven't done them but they  should be as familiar tools as imposing  sanctions so does the chessboard and the  web ever come together at any point or  do they always remain separate in terms  of solving problems that's a really good  question and and over they certainly  will come together for one thing they'll  conflict if you're trying to build a  network with a country's entrepreneurs  and you need to sanction or you need to  do something that you know isolate them  diplomatically the people you're trying  to network with will respond to the way  you're treating their governments so  there's always going to be trade-offs in  various ways I would say though for most  people this is more like parallel play  one thing the network strategies are  always longer-term  and it's actually very important to put  them in a in place ahead of time so a  good example would be lets say you wanna  you've got a very fragile state there's  been a lot of ethnic tension you want a  network for monitoring violence early on  well you can't put that in place once  there's a crisis in that country that's  too late so the the chessboard  strategies tend to be the short term a  crisis is it what do we do the web  strategies tend to be the longer term  think about how you do this in such a  way that they they have value and that  when the crisis hits they can be  activated so that's why I say I think  you need to I call it seeing in stereo  okay so what about some of the other  problems of the world for instance if we  look at the Syrian refugees for instance  what what what do you offer in terms of  that in terms of solutions web or the  word the chessboard I'm probably a  little both maybe your little both it's  so one for one thing like so Europe is  trying to decide what Europe's policy is  toward refugees that's for European  governments to work out or the United  Nations and the United Nations agencies  trying to help refugees that's the  chessboard I mean it's States coming  together 